WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
Hi it's me Michelle from Lab Muffin
Beauty Science possibly the nerdiest

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:08.940
beauty blogger / eatbeast on the Internet.
Today I'm going to be sharing my routine

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.820
for getting rid of post acne marks or
post inflammatory hyperpigmentation or

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:24.449
PIH. If you have dark skin or if you have
Asian or African genes you probably have

00:00:24.449 --> 00:00:29.010
to deal with post inflammatory
hyperpigmentation. This is when your skin

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:32.399
decides to go into pigment production
overdrive after you have inflammation

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:38.489
like wounds, pimples or sunburn. This
leaves dark marks on your skin that can

00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:44.340
be light brown or purpley black. PIH is
really annoying because it's like after

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.100
the pimple leaves
its annoying shadow lingers and lingers

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:51.860
and lingers for weeks and weeks and
weeks. Different things work for

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.620
different people but here's what I found
works really well for my post

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:00.239
inflammatory hyperpigmentation. It might
work for you as well, let me know if it

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:02.420
does.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:09.070
A good sunscreen makes a huge difference
for fading my pigment. In particular I

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:14.170
found that switching to a sunscreen that
has high UVA protection helps a lot. I

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:18.550
would say that sunscreen is probably the
number one thing that's faded my pigment.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:23.409
UVA is longer wavelengths of UV that
tend to cause more photoaging, so

00:01:23.409 --> 00:01:28.060
wrinkles, pigmentation - than burning,
which is mostly caused by shorter

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:32.800
wavelengths in the UVB region. Most
sunscreens are really good at blocking

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:41.200
UVB, which is mostly measured by SPF, so
SPF is 80 to 90% caused by UVB. The

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.890
dangers of UVB have been known for ages
but UVA is a relatively new development

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:51.310
so not all sunscreens are good at
blocking UVA yet, because of the

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:56.289
regulations which haven't changed that
much for many years. And in particular a

00:01:56.289 --> 00:02:00.220
lot of sunscreens aren't that good at
blocking the longest wavelengths of UVA

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:06.670
which is known as UVA1. Annoyingly
longer UVA wavelengths do still cause

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:10.000
pigmentation. There aren't that many
ingredients that are good at blocking

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:15.280
longer wavelengths of UVA. In the US
there's really only avobenzone and

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:20.620
zinc oxide, which in my opinion both kind
of suck. Avobenzones really good at

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:25.420
blocking UVA but it's not photostable
which means that after you've exposed it

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.590
to too much UV light - after it's absorbed
too much and turnéd that into heat - it's

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:33.910
going to start breaking down, and once
it's broken down it's not going to

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:38.739
absorb UV anymore, so your skin's no
longer protected. The amount for avobenzone

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:43.390
is really quite low, so if it's by
itself in a sunscreen it turns out that

00:02:43.390 --> 00:02:47.980
it's mostly broken down after about an
hour or two. There are ingredients that

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:53.459
can help stabilise avobenzone in
sunscreens but still it's not ideal so

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:59.379
this is part of the reason why they tell
you to reapply your sunscreen. Zinc oxide

00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:04.180
on the other hand is photostable but it
doesn't block UVA very well and in

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.680
general you can't really get a PPD or
UVA protection factor of more than

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:13.570
around 20. It's also more prone to issues
like clumping on your skin after it's

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:16.510
been there for too long and if it's
clumped up that means it's not

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:21.700
spread out evenly and so sunlight can
get in between those zinc oxide clumps.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:27.370
In other countries like Europe (I know it's not a country) and Japan
and Australia where I am there are more

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:32.019
options for sunscreens that can block
UVA1. There are actually sunscreens

00:03:32.019 --> 00:03:37.230
that can get PPD values of over 40 so
this is similar to SPF except for

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:42.730
something called persistent pigment
darkening which happens with UVA. The

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:45.909
sunscreen ingredients that can help
sunscreens get to these really really

00:03:45.909 --> 00:03:51.760
high UVA protection factors are newer
photostable organic sunscreens - in other

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:57.730
words they are so-called chemical
sunscreens. Some of these are Tinosorb S

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:04.209
and Tinosorb M, Mexoryl SX which is
allowed in the US, Mexoryl XL,

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:10.480
and Uvinul A Plus. Unfortunately these
haven't been approved in the US yet but

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.310
you can easily get these sunscreens
online. My favorite ingredients have been

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:20.350
Tinosorb S and M, and Tinosorb S is
actually in my favorite sunscreen for

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:26.440
daily use which is  Ultraceuticals
Ultra UV Daily Moisturiser SPF 30. Even

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:30.820
though it's only SPF 30 the texture is
really really nice and it spreads and

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:35.500
dries really easily so I can apply a lot
of this sunscreen. And since sunscreen

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.460
protection scales with how much you
apply I probably end up getting more

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:44.770
protection with this SPF 30 sunscreen
than I would with a thicker greasy SPF

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:49.479
50 sunscreen. Most sunscreens also clog
my pores which gives me more acne and

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:54.070
therefore more hyperpigmentation, but
this one doesn't. It also works really

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:59.940
really well under my makeup, it doesn't
boil up or anything like that.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.340
I don't know what I'd do for PIH if I
didn't have vitamin C. It does a bunch of

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:10.000
things for pigmentation. The first step
in producing melanin pigment in your

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.890
body is turning the amino acid tyrosine
into l-dopa which is then turned into

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:19.300
dopaquinone. The dopaquinone
eventually turns into melanin. The enzyme

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:24.730
tyrosinase does both of these steps - it's
like a tiny factory. Vitamin c undoes the

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:29.500
second reaction so it slows down pigment
production. Vitamin C is also a really

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:34.510
really good antioxidant when UVA
interacts with skin it forms reactive

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:39.250
substances with unpaired electrons
called free radicals. These free radicals

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:42.250
are super reactive and they're so
reactive that they'll start reacting

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:47.320
willy-nilly with things in your skin - so
things like DNA, proteins and lipids. And

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:52.030
in this process it damages them and this
process, which is sometimes called

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:58.360
oxidative stress, leads to pigmentation.
Vitamin C reacts with free radicals and

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:03.280
stabilizes them so they can't react with
and damage other substances. This means

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:07.150
that you get less new pigmentation which
means that your skin is able to heal

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:11.650
existing pigmentation, and this happens
with the sunscreen as well. I'm quite

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:16.660
basic with my vitamin C so my favorite
vitamin C product is just a DIY vitamin

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:22.480
C serum that I make fresh with ascorbic
acid powder, water and baking soda, and I

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:26.860
have to remake it every week because
firstly the vitamin C isn't stabilized

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:30.610
so starts breaking down quite quickly,
and it's also doesn't have a

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:34.000
preservative. And while it's acidic
enough that it probably doesn't need a

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.590
preservative I try to stay on the safe
side. Sometimes I get really lazy and I

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:42.070
just mix a pinch of the vitamin C powder
with the moisturizer right before I

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:46.780
apply it on my face - which works for me
because my skin is quite used to acid

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:51.370
and so it's not that sensitive to things
like that, but if your skin isn't quite

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:55.840
as used to acid, proceed with massive
caution because again, irritating your

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.790
skin can cause more pigmentation.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:06.870
I've talked about my love of lactic acid
before in my acid ingredients video. It's

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:11.130
an alpha hydroxy acid that's a little
bit larger than your standard glycolic

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:14.340
acid and because it's slightly larger
that means it's going to be a bit

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:18.539
gentler because it's harder for it to
get into the skin quite as quickly. It's

00:07:18.539 --> 00:07:22.199
also been found to be better at
hydrating and better at treating photo

00:07:22.199 --> 00:07:27.419
damage in some experiments. I like
glycolic acid as well but lactic acid

00:07:27.419 --> 00:07:31.319
just happens to work better for me. I've
also been trying out mandelic acid

00:07:31.319 --> 00:07:36.000
lately as well. Since these alpha hydroxy
acids are good for things apart from

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:40.650
just hyperpigmentation - so they exfoliate
and they prevent acne as well - I tend to

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:44.280
use them all the time, but I try to use
them more consistently and more

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:48.150
regularly when I'm dealing with post
inflammatory hyperpigmentation. My

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:52.320
favorite lactic acid products are
probably Kate Somerville's ExfoliKate

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:57.360
and Ultraceuticals Brightening Serum.
ExfoliKate is a really good product for

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.460
people like me who are ambitious but
kind of lazy and you sort of forget to

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:06.870
do your alpha hydroxy acids for a bit.
It's a mask that you use in the shower

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:11.639
for about two minutes and it acts like a
peel. It's particularly good because it

00:08:11.639 --> 00:08:16.199
comes out as a thick paste so it doesn't
drip around all over the place and so it

00:08:16.199 --> 00:08:20.130
stays on your skin in the shower. I
actually tend to leave it on for a

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:25.229
little bit more than two minutes because
I slowly increased my exposure to lactic

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:28.259
acid so I can tell that my skin can
handle it

00:08:28.259 --> 00:08:32.070
I wouldn't recommend doing this unless
you're quite experienced with acids and

00:08:32.070 --> 00:08:37.140
how acids act on your skin and I'd start
off really slowly following the actual

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:41.370
instructions before building to this
sort of exposure. The danger with going

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:45.540
too quickly with strong acid products is
something that a lot of people call

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:50.790
damaging the moisture barrier which is
when you use too much acid and you end

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:56.010
up giving yourself kind of like a mild
chemical burn. Your skin ends up quite

00:08:56.010 --> 00:09:01.770
sensitive and stings and whenever you
use actives they end up just irritating

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:06.660
your skin quite a lot and it doesn't
help your skin recover. To wait for your

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.870
skin to recover you kind of have to
treat it really really gently and baby

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:14.520
it for weeks and so don't go
too hard into acids without a lot of

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.860
caution. But if you know what you're
doing

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:21.540
Exfolikate is really really good. It makes
my skin look smooth and glowy like I've

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:26.160
actually been consistently using AHAs for like weeks and weeks. It's a

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:30.450
little bit pricey though but a little
does go a long way with this product. You

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:34.320
can put a really thin film on your face
especially if you're doing it in the

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:39.540
shower. Try to get a sample of it if you
can, I use a little pencil eraser size

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:45.210
amount each time. I also really like
Ultraceutials Brightening Serum. It's

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:49.140
quite pricey but it was a key part of
this Ultraceuticals routine that I did

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:54.450
last year and my pigment faded tons and
tons during that process and so I just

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:58.170
don't really want to mess with this
winning formula for my skin, at least

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:02.730
until I run out and I have to repurchase
it. I've also tried a few other lactic

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:07.050
acid products that are a bit cheaper.
Tarte Knockout Tingling Treatment is one

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:11.340
that I've recently tried and it's been
really really good. The Ordinary have

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:16.050
some lactic acids. I've tried the 10% and
it's theoretically quite good but it

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:19.680
didn't seem to be quite as effective
when I used it, although I didn't really

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:24.720
use it as consistently as I wanted and I
didn't really monitor my skin that

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:29.670
closely, so maybe it does work better. It
could also just work better for you

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:33.870
because of your skin's individual needs.
I've also been trying out mandelic more

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:38.130
lately and it's been really good but
mandelic acid products are just a lot

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:44.490
harder to get. So Stratia Soft Touch AHA is probably the

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:48.930
most accessible one, it has 10% mandelic
acid and it comes in this really handy

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:53.160
pump bottle and in general if skincare
comes in a pump I'm gonna like it a lot

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:57.390
more because I'm quite lazy. So these
three things - sunscreen

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:02.700
vitamin C and lactic acid - if I use all
of these ingredients quite consistently

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:07.440
my PIH usually fades dramatically in the
first two weeks or so after the acne's

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:12.090
gone down, and it goes down enough that
it's covered with just my usual medium

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:16.230
coverage foundation and I actually don't
need concealer. Everyone's skin is

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:20.010
different so your fading process, your
skin's healing processes probably won't

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:24.960
be the same. It might heal faster or
slower but this is what works for me and

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:30.450
the evidence does
show that it does work so give it a try

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:34.410
maybe it'll work for you as well. Thanks
for watching this video, if you liked it

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:39.300
please subscribe to my channel, you can
also check out my blog, you can also

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:43.620
follow me on Facebook and Instagram
where I post lots of beauty tips. See you

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:47.180
next time for more beauty science

